      "problem": "Simplify $\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}}.$",
      "worked_solution": "We first write $20{n}^{5}$ as the product $4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n$ which contains a perfect square $(4{n}^{4}).$ Then, we can separate the product into two separate radicals, one of which can be simplified:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{20{n}^{5}} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{4{n}^{4}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}\\cdot 5n} & = \\\\ \\sqrt{(2{n}^{2})^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ |2|\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nA squared number is always positive or zero, and the absolute value of any positive (or zero) number is itself, so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\cdot |{n}^{2}|\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2\\cdot {n}^{2}\\cdot \\sqrt{5n} & = \\\\ 2{n}^{2}\\sqrt{5n}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
